00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:40.100
Now, one myth I want us to work on
today is this idea of a meritocracy.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:47.470
Many of our workplaces are designed
around striving to be a meritocracy.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:51.912
So the idea of a meritocracy is
that everyone is evaluated for

00:04:51.912 --> 00:04:57.860
the merit that they bring to the company,
the project, to the team.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:03.600
And that every idea Is truly evaluated
based on the merit of that idea.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.560
We hold that belief widely.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:12.706
But research shows there's a lot of errors
that we make that prevent us from truly

00:05:12.706 --> 00:05:17.624
seeing talent and ideas equally depending
on the composition of the person,

00:05:17.624 --> 00:05:21.350
the identity of the person,
who's presenting the idea.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:27.566
So, meritocracy is a belief,
it's a myth, that all people and

00:05:27.566 --> 00:05:31.360
all ideas are evaluated based on merit.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.840
Now, here's a really interesting finding.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.390
Emilio Castillo and Steven Bernard,
Emilio got his PhD here at Stanford.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:43.831
They did a research on a company,
about a 5000 person company.

00:05:43.831 --> 00:05:48.030
And they went to the leaders and said,
what would you like to accomplish?

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:52.330
And they said we want to deliver
on this promise of meritocracy.

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:57.847
We believe a meritocracy is when we
very carefully evaluate performance.

00:05:57.847 --> 00:06:00.196
And then when we give
out salary increases.

00:06:00.196 --> 00:06:06.108
The performance correlates
to the salary increase.

00:06:06.108 --> 00:06:11.650
So they worked with this organization,
had them do that, they were very proud.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:17.970
By the end, they felt that they had truly
correlated performance, and pay increases.

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:21.800
And then the second phase was
the assignment of a merit bonus.

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:22.836
And as you know,

00:06:22.836 --> 00:06:28.030
merit bonus is a one time bonus that's
given based on your performance.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:31.741
So they asked the managers to
hand out merit bonuses, and

00:06:31.741 --> 00:06:36.550
then they evaluated them, the researchers
did based on gender and race.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.533
And what they discovered was that the
merit bonus was not linked to performance

00:06:42.533 --> 00:06:44.050
rating.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:48.258
But gender and race was more
predictive of how much you got.

00:06:48.258 --> 00:06:53.533
And what they discovered is that if we
believe in this myth of meritocracy,

00:06:53.533 --> 00:06:57.020
we are less likely to
scrutinize our behaviors.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:01.461
So when managers work for what they
believe are a meritocratic organization,

00:07:01.461 --> 00:07:05.480
they believe that they
are the ones who are meritocratic.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:08.490
And what happens is,
they stop scrutinizing their behavior.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:12.956
So if all of us have what
we call cognitive biases,

00:07:12.956 --> 00:07:17.444
where we make errors based on
stereotypes about types of people,

00:07:17.444 --> 00:07:22.747
if we all do that and we stop scrutinizing
because of the myth of meritocracy,

00:07:22.747 --> 00:07:27.152
research shows that we are likely
to be more biased then if we have

00:07:27.152 --> 00:07:31.180
concerns that we're not good,
and check our behaviors.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:38.770
So how do you debunk
this idea of meritocracy?

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:43.750
One is there's a lot of research that
shows how these errors take place.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:48.101
This one I really like it's in
the field of the legal scholars.

00:07:48.101 --> 00:07:54.144
And they wanted to convince lawyers that
that bias existed even in the legal field.

00:07:54.144 --> 00:07:59.040
And the lawyer said, well now,
our job is to find the truth.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.520
We're really good at looking through
something and finding the truth.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:05.860
There are biases in the legal field.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:10.270
So what the researcher did was created
a legal brief and put errors in it.

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:13.540
Spelling, theoretical, grammatical errors.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:19.763
Sent it to law partners with the request
to evaluate their third year law student.

00:08:19.763 --> 00:08:22.902
And in it,
they simply wrote in the cover letter for

00:08:22.902 --> 00:08:26.562
some that he is white and
some that he's African American.

00:08:26.562 --> 00:08:30.046
And any lawyers in the house,
you don't have to admit it.

00:08:30.046 --> 00:08:36.500
I've spoken to lawyers and they say,
who writes a brief doesn't matter.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:41.823
A brief is a brief, it stands on its
own merit, yet what they discovered

00:08:41.823 --> 00:08:47.328
is that when the lawyers thought that
Thomas Meyer was African American,

00:08:47.328 --> 00:08:52.962
they found those mistakes that the
researchers had put in the legal brief.

00:08:52.962 --> 00:08:58.829
Now notice that they didn't find them
regularly when they thought he was white.

00:08:58.829 --> 00:09:01.155
Now why would that be?

00:09:01.155 --> 00:09:07.263
The historical archetype of success
affects how we evaluate things.

00:09:07.263 --> 00:09:12.298
In the past, there haven't been
a lot of black legal partners.

00:09:12.298 --> 00:09:17.346
So despite the fact that for example,
Michelle Obama is a very well known

00:09:17.346 --> 00:09:23.720
Black Legal partner, that hasn't affected
our stereotypes or how we see success.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:28.940
So whoever doesn't match who we expect
to be there just gets more scrutiny.

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:33.495
And not only more scrutiny, but
then he black Thomas Meyer got

00:09:33.495 --> 00:09:37.890
a rating of 3.2 and
we can't even believe he went to NYU.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:43.531
And White Thomas Meyer has potential,
got a 4.1.

00:09:43.531 --> 00:09:47.460
And this is where the myth of
meritocracy starts to fall apart.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.491
We do these studies in countries all over,
right?

00:09:50.491 --> 00:09:54.090
Europe, Asia, where they change
one thing in a resume and

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:57.070
they send it to actual hiring managers.

00:09:57.070 --> 00:10:00.470
And they see how actual
hiring managers respond.

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:04.360
So we just talked about,
how race can affect evaluations.

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:07.456
In the UK what they did
was in some resumes,

00:10:07.456 --> 00:10:13.477
said that the person had been a member of
a Gay Pride organization while in college,

00:10:13.477 --> 00:10:17.194
that one had to send in more
resumes to get Collins.

00:10:17.194 --> 00:10:21.222
The second one in Germany,
you can put a picture with your CV,

00:10:21.222 --> 00:10:23.610
the exact same picture.

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:26.694
Sometimes they'd have a German name,
sometimes a Turkish name, and

00:10:26.694 --> 00:10:28.760
sometimes Turkish with a head scarf.

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:30.700
And that was the least
likely to get called in.

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:35.036
So you could say ethnic national origin or
religious.

00:10:35.036 --> 00:10:38.020
And then the last one was
the motherhood penalty I referred to.

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:38.963
So.

00:10:38.963 --> 00:10:44.780
A resume says a person was officer
in a Parent Teacher Association.

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:47.840
It's called unless,
that resume is called unless.

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:52.415
Not only that, but
the candidate is rated less qualified,

00:10:52.415 --> 00:10:55.630
less loyal, and offered a lower salary.

00:10:57.260 --> 00:11:01.169
Now they did the same with two
men's resumes they put officer of

00:11:01.169 --> 00:11:05.096
a parent teacher association at
the bottom of a man's resume.

00:11:05.096 --> 00:11:09.883
It was called and more often rated,
higher more competent and more loyal.

00:11:09.883 --> 00:11:13.940
So they call this the motherhood
penalty and the breadwinner bonus.

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:21.953
Now, I'm going to see how we're going to
do with our own automatic associations and

00:11:21.953 --> 00:11:25.871
if you wouldn't mind calling out,
picture genius.

00:11:25.871 --> 00:11:31.979
And then, who's willing to
tell me the name of a genius?

00:11:31.979 --> 00:11:34.893
Is anybody, please?

00:11:34.893 --> 00:11:36.774
&gt;&gt; Maradona.

00:11:36.774 --> 00:11:38.327
&gt;&gt; Madame
&gt;&gt; Maradona.

00:11:38.327 --> 00:11:39.383
&gt;&gt; Maradona?

00:11:39.383 --> 00:11:42.761
The same.

00:11:42.761 --> 00:11:47.456
Emmanuelle Miranda.

00:11:47.456 --> 00:11:48.336
&gt;&gt; Lin Manuel.

00:11:48.336 --> 00:11:49.780
&gt;&gt; Lin Manuel Miranda.

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:51.107
Anyone else?

00:11:58.067 --> 00:12:07.226
Einstein, Claims

00:12:07.226 --> 00:12:08.700
to have the highest IQ.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:15.478
So somebody who has the highest
IQ would be a genius.

00:12:15.478 --> 00:12:19.070
So we have artistic geniuses,
we have mathematical geniuses,

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:20.807
we have scientific geniuses.

00:12:20.807 --> 00:12:25.754
In general, we tend to pick men as genius,
in general, right?

00:12:25.754 --> 00:12:29.430
In general when I do this,
people in general say Einstein,

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:31.057
which you threw me a bone.

00:12:31.057 --> 00:12:33.701
Thank you very much, appreciate it.

00:12:33.701 --> 00:12:38.355
And what's hidden from view are often,
women, people of color who

00:12:38.355 --> 00:12:43.860
are equally qualified in the exact
same fields, are hidden from view.

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:47.103
So what happens in our brains
is that when historical

00:12:47.103 --> 00:12:50.800
archetypes are in words like genius,
it narrows our view.

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:56.890
And we ended up only seeing who
matches that historical archetype.

00:12:56.890 --> 00:13:03.370
And even though there are a lot of other
folks there, we tend not to notice them.

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:07.729
If you saw when Forbes
launched its very first 100

00:13:07.729 --> 00:13:11.296
most innovative leaders in America list,

00:13:11.296 --> 00:13:17.357
there was a big Twitter storm because
the list had 99 men and one woman.

00:13:17.357 --> 00:13:21.474
Now, when Forbes responded to this list,
they said,

00:13:21.474 --> 00:13:25.865
well we did use a methodology,
but clearly it was flawed.

00:13:25.865 --> 00:13:29.522
So if you're not seeking
to catch whether your

00:13:29.522 --> 00:13:34.780
methodology has these errors in
it likely you are enacting it.

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:38.959
And that's how we try to debunk
these myths of the workplace.

00:13:38.959 --> 00:13:43.530
By showing that these commonly held
beliefs are false, but widely held, and

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:46.480
we can debunk them with some
of the data that is done

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:49.250
here at the business
goal is being conducted.

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:56.100
Now in this study which was in 2019,
they worked with the group of scientists.

00:13:56.100 --> 00:14:00.839
And they had them take that test to see if
they had bias against women in science and

00:14:00.839 --> 00:14:04.672
they discovered that pretty
universally just like in the world,

00:14:04.672 --> 00:14:09.470
people have bias that men are more
likely to be scientists than women.

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:11.480
That's not what differentiated them.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:15.582
What differentiated the group was when
they asked why do you think there are so

00:14:15.582 --> 00:14:16.790
few women in science?

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:21.920
And the ones that said, I don't know
science isn't sexist, we're empirical.

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:24.870
We're putting in one group.

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:28.290
And another group said, I don't know
either, but I know barriers exist.

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:31.310
And then they left them alone.

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:33.820
And two years later went and
looked at who they hired.

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:39.499
And it turns out the group that
acknowledged that barriers existed ended

00:14:39.499 --> 00:14:41.500
up hiring more women.

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:44.672
And the ones that said,
I don't know science isn't sexist,

00:14:44.672 --> 00:14:47.660
we're empirical ended up not hiring women.

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:52.032
So whether you believe these myths or
not, whether you seek to debunk them

00:14:52.032 --> 00:14:56.755
can affect your behaviors in ways that
reinforce the myths or can debunk them and

00:14:56.755 --> 00:14:58.820
come up with different outcomes.

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:03.640
So when I think about the difference,
it's not intention.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:09.580
Scientists in both of
those groups had biases.

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:16.000
And in most cases people want
to pick the best person.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:21.200
Very few people I talk to say, I hope I
pick the second best candidate, right?

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:26.024
But the difference or the actions
we take to block these dynamics,

00:15:26.024 --> 00:15:29.653
these myths from affecting
our decision making.

00:15:29.653 --> 00:15:34.594
And that's where I get to rituals,
so again I looked up the definition

00:15:34.594 --> 00:15:39.640
of ritual it's an act or
series of acts regularly repeated.

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:42.869
So that's the thing about myths,
if we do something once,

00:15:42.869 --> 00:15:45.410
it doesn't make the myth go away.

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:50.218
We have to regularly repeat actions
that block the myths from affecting

00:15:50.218 --> 00:15:54.236
our decision making to have
any sort of different outcome.

00:15:54.236 --> 00:15:57.610
And in a way, I think about
about honoring who people are.

00:15:57.610 --> 00:16:01.870
I think about it as a ritual which
seems very much like a process.

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:05.958
This is actually trying to honor
the person I'm trying to evaluate,

00:16:05.958 --> 00:16:10.050
really trying to see who they
are beyond these automatic responses.

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:16.020
So one way to debunk this
myth of the ideal worker,

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:19.568
to remember the ideal
worker is the myth that.

00:16:19.568 --> 00:16:21.234
People who have work and

00:16:21.234 --> 00:16:27.220
focus on work at work are more productive
than those who have home concerns.

00:16:27.220 --> 00:16:32.184
One way to debunk the myth of the ideal
worker is by having regular check

00:16:32.184 --> 00:16:33.120
ins at work.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:37.245
Some really interesting work
done by Leslie Pirlo at

00:16:37.245 --> 00:16:41.683
the Harvard Business School she was
studying the Boston Consulting Group.

00:16:41.683 --> 00:16:46.289
And they were having issues because people
were feeling like they had no control

00:16:46.289 --> 00:16:51.650
over their lives, and therefore this
work life, integration was failing.

00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:57.860
So they instituted what they called
one day off predictably a week.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:03.450
Now, if you're on a consulting project, it
meant even if you're in a different state,

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:06.440
you were off the project for
one evening a week.

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:12.972
It meant if you were buying tickets
to a show or you're trying to see

00:17:12.972 --> 00:17:19.250
your kid's game that you knew that
night off was truly that night off.

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:22.350
Well, the teams end up
being more productive.

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:28.696
Not because that one night off was
necessarily rejuvenative although it was,

00:17:28.696 --> 00:17:31.200
it increased retention.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:36.344
But interestingly, because they had to
coordinate across how their personal lives

00:17:36.344 --> 00:17:42.170
worked, they ended up communicating
even more strongly about their projects.

00:17:42.170 --> 00:17:46.778
So some people argue we don't have time
for personal check ins we're about work.

00:17:46.778 --> 00:17:49.676
Work is work and
personal life is personal.

00:17:49.676 --> 00:17:54.378
But there's the chance that when you
integrate them through a simple check in

00:17:54.378 --> 00:17:58.211
at the beginning of the meeting,
you can increase both work and

00:17:58.211 --> 00:17:59.822
personal satisfaction.

00:17:59.822 --> 00:18:05.984
By doing that regularly,
it can change the tone of work over time,

00:18:05.984 --> 00:18:10.180
as Pirlo saw in
the Boston Consulting Group.

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:15.926
A ritual can also be as simple as agree
to criteria before you make a decision.

00:18:15.926 --> 00:18:20.587
If you remember that brief where
the valuation of it shifted based

00:18:20.587 --> 00:18:25.587
unconsciously on the stereotype of
the person evaluating it, well,

00:18:25.587 --> 00:18:29.750
research shows that we
will shift the criteria.

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:33.208
So if we recall it was
the exact same legal brief, but

00:18:33.208 --> 00:18:36.780
the criteria shifted in the valuation.

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:41.213
It turns out if you simply think
carefully about how you're going to

00:18:41.213 --> 00:18:45.590
evaluate someone before you do,
you're less likely to shift it.

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:51.170
So for example, in the legal brief,
someone had said,

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:56.760
did you scrutinize for
spelling mistakes equally in both briefs?

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:01.644
Likely the ones who looked at the one
from white Thomas Meyer didn't check that

00:19:01.644 --> 00:19:02.390
very well.

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:08.810
Sadly, this happened to me once at work,
where and we had candidates and

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:10.980
all of a sudden, the top candidate,

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:15.680
someone said, there are a lot of
spelling mistakes in their cover letter.

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:17.354
We didn't really notice it.

00:19:17.354 --> 00:19:21.837
But I think someone who's going to be in
our role really needs to write well for

00:19:21.837 --> 00:19:23.540
the public audience.

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:26.290
We really can't have spelling mistakes.

00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:29.970
And so we thought, we should
probably discount that candidate.

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:33.926
What I didn't do and
I will regret forever is that I didn't say

00:19:33.926 --> 00:19:38.279
did you equally check for
spelling mistakes in all the candidates?

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:43.823
Because if we had, I'm sure we
would have found a similar number.

00:19:43.823 --> 00:19:46.324
Because we did it we ended up saying this,
okay,

00:19:46.324 --> 00:19:49.410
we took the person off the qualified list.

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:53.016
So simply is saying if you're going
to hold someone accountable for

00:19:53.016 --> 00:19:56.640
the way they're communicating or
the way they're writing.

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:01.633
Did you really check to see, that that was
equally scrutinized on every applicant?

00:20:03.751 --> 00:20:09.050
Now, has anyone made a New Year's
resolution that didn't stick?

00:20:10.060 --> 00:20:10.740
Kind of thing, yeah.

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:16.798
And in fact, a lot of people make
New Year's resolutions around eating like,

00:20:16.798 --> 00:20:21.749
I don't know about you, but do you notice
I'm wearing this little top that's looser

00:20:21.749 --> 00:20:23.642
on the bottom than it is up here.

00:20:23.642 --> 00:20:26.925
This is what I call COVID clothing, right,

00:20:26.925 --> 00:20:30.830
you got the nice snug part
where it doesn't count and

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:35.477
then a nice loose part where
the COVID effect has taken place.

00:20:35.477 --> 00:20:42.690
So lots of people have given me advice
about what to do about COVID clothing.

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:44.163
And interestingly,

00:20:44.163 --> 00:20:49.210
a lot of the things you're supposed
to not do make me want to do it more.

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:49.950
Have you ever tried that?

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:54.123
Like, I'm only going to drink wine on the
weekends, and then I have a really tough

00:20:54.123 --> 00:20:58.630
meeting, like tonight I'll just have one
glass, not as a staff wineglass, right?

00:20:58.630 --> 00:21:04.460
So there's ways that telling me not to do
something makes me want to do it more.

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:09.777
So it turns out in behavioral economics,
there's this idea of a nudge and

00:21:09.777 --> 00:21:13.810
nudge doesn't tell you
not to do something.

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:17.570
But a nudge encourages you
to do the right thing.

00:21:17.570 --> 00:21:19.621
It constrains your choices,

00:21:19.621 --> 00:21:24.750
so you end up doing the right thing
without having to remember to.

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:28.500
Now, a good example,
is anyone not from the United States here?

00:21:29.980 --> 00:21:32.640
Are you shocked about how
big our servings are?

00:21:34.050 --> 00:21:40.686
It's like you could feed a family
off of one plate of food seriously.

00:21:40.686 --> 00:21:44.293
I went to this one meal and
I can't believe I was eating it all and

00:21:44.293 --> 00:21:48.875
in reflection it was the amount of food
that we usually buy for my family of four.

00:21:48.875 --> 00:21:53.199
But I ended up eating it because it was
on this really beautiful plate, and

00:21:53.199 --> 00:21:54.121
we were there.

00:21:54.121 --> 00:21:59.309
And so it turns out, the plate size in
the United States has increased over time,

00:21:59.309 --> 00:22:03.630
similarly to a lot of the ways
that we started to eat more.

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:10.620
So, if you simply eat off a smaller plate,
it's not telling me Laurie don't eat more.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:14.203
It's just saying like how much
fits on this little plate.

00:22:14.203 --> 00:22:17.935
So one way to help people with good
behavior is not to tell them don't

00:22:17.935 --> 00:22:18.530
eat more.

00:22:18.530 --> 00:22:23.231
It's really to figure out, you've heard
it, smaller plates, healthy sizes,

00:22:23.231 --> 00:22:25.080
eat slowly, all those things.

00:22:26.150 --> 00:22:31.888
Okay, so it turns out if you in your
work are making decisions about

00:22:31.888 --> 00:22:38.561
people without some sort of scorecard,
likely you are making these shifts.

00:22:38.561 --> 00:22:44.465
Even if your company has some
performance evaluation or

00:22:44.465 --> 00:22:51.762
hiring platform if you as a leader
require that everyone do an input.

00:22:51.762 --> 00:22:56.600
They're evaluations into a scorecard,
you're going to get less of the shifts and

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:57.440
evaluation.

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:04.919
If you in your agendas just regularly put
that check in at first it will be awkward.

00:23:04.919 --> 00:23:09.508
But over time, you'll get more
accustomed to integrating work and

00:23:09.508 --> 00:23:12.150
life and have less of a barrier.

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:17.113
Even during COVID a lot of women told
me there was understanding about them

00:23:17.113 --> 00:23:21.530
having kids kind of running
through their Zoom screen.

00:23:21.530 --> 00:23:24.947
And yet when we ask about promotions and
opportunities and

00:23:24.947 --> 00:23:29.771
how they feel their advancement is going
they still feel like it was a constraint.

00:23:29.771 --> 00:23:35.590
One thing a check in can do is help reduce
that myth that people with things going

00:23:35.590 --> 00:23:41.160
on outside of work are less productive
than those who focus solely on work.

00:23:43.610 --> 00:23:46.360
And then I love this idea
of changing the default.

00:23:47.620 --> 00:23:52.683
So it turns out that unbeknownst to us,
we have defaults

00:23:52.683 --> 00:23:59.149
about the way life works if you think
about for example parental leave,

00:23:59.149 --> 00:24:04.830
often it's structured
around biological parents.

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:09.610
Which, in some organizations they
have to rewrite it to include, for

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:13.345
example, adoptive parents or
step parents, right?

00:24:13.345 --> 00:24:16.815
So the default is a biological parent,

00:24:16.815 --> 00:24:23.130
by shifting that it creates
more inclusion organizations.

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:27.702
Well, the way we talk also has a lot
of defaults, so if you say for

00:24:27.702 --> 00:24:32.560
example, I want to welcome all
the couples and the same sex couples.

00:24:33.770 --> 00:24:38.752
What I'm saying is the default
is a one man, one woman couple,

00:24:38.752 --> 00:24:41.580
and everyone else is an exception.

00:24:42.720 --> 00:24:47.542
So our words have ways of again
indicating who is more included

00:24:47.542 --> 00:24:51.170
in our organizations and
who is an exception?

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:56.892
And if you've noticed around Stanford,
a lot of us will put our

00:24:56.892 --> 00:25:01.476
pronouns on our zoom, IDs, or
when we work with undergrads,

00:25:01.476 --> 00:25:07.120
it's often incorporated into
how they introduce each other.

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:10.619
Now, why is it important that
everyone shares their pronouns?

00:25:11.660 --> 00:25:16.969
Well, it shifts the default, the myth
is that there's a default, and everyone

00:25:16.969 --> 00:25:22.970
else is the exception but by shifting
the default we're debunking that myth.

00:25:22.970 --> 00:25:28.080
So by sharing my pronouns, my name is
Laurie Mackenzie, I use she her pronouns.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:32.944
I'm saying the default is that we all
might have different pronouns than

00:25:32.944 --> 00:25:38.340
you expect, and therefore it's just
the way it is that we share them.

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:43.248
So in your workplaces as well, there
may be defaults that are unconsciously

00:25:43.248 --> 00:25:48.001
being reinforced, where there's
something without a descriptor, and

00:25:48.001 --> 00:25:50.870
everyone else gets a descriptor.

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:56.920
And the question is, could you catch those
and make it so that everyone is included?

00:25:56.920 --> 00:25:59.110
Not a default and an exception.

00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:04.037
Okay, now what have we done
here at the business school,

00:26:04.037 --> 00:26:09.190
around some of these myths and some of the
ways to create a more inclusive campus?

00:26:09.190 --> 00:26:13.594
And if you didn't know we've been
publishing a diversity equity inclusion

00:26:13.594 --> 00:26:18.660
report for the past few years, this is
our third report which just came out.

00:26:18.660 --> 00:26:22.100
And in all honesty,
we keep shifting when we publish it so

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:25.969
it's not like you would know it
comes out a certain time of year,

00:26:25.969 --> 00:26:29.698
we rely on central Stanford data
about our representation and

00:26:29.698 --> 00:26:34.239
we are kind of working out when that
comes in and then when we write a report.

00:26:34.239 --> 00:26:38.850
But I wanted to share a little bit
with you about how we've tried

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:41.645
to be more inclusive in the work we do.

00:26:41.645 --> 00:26:44.388
So one of the myths that
we as a university,

00:26:44.388 --> 00:26:49.420
as people need to debunk is that well,
everyone can be their authentic self and

00:26:49.420 --> 00:26:54.320
there's lots of data that shows that's
not true, equally for everybody.

00:26:55.540 --> 00:27:00.053
So one thing Stanford University
did was they conducted

00:27:00.053 --> 00:27:04.469
the largest scale culture
survey ever in our history,

00:27:04.469 --> 00:27:10.517
President Tessier Lavigne launched it
after the murder of George Floyd and

00:27:10.517 --> 00:27:17.260
said he wanted us to understand how race
affected how people felt here at Stanford.

00:27:17.260 --> 00:27:22.444
Almost 15,000 people answered this survey,
it's the very first survey we've

00:27:22.444 --> 00:27:27.414
ever conducted across students, staff and
faculty, exact same survey, because

00:27:27.414 --> 00:27:32.473
what we wanted to understand was how is it
that people are experiencing the culture.

00:27:32.473 --> 00:27:38.240
Is that myth true that everybody can
equally be their authentic selves?

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:42.570
This survey you can find it online they've
put all the results to be available

00:27:42.570 --> 00:27:47.040
because transparency also helps debunk
myths giving everyone access to data.

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:53.036
What they discovered was that race and
sexual orientation,

00:27:53.036 --> 00:27:57.873
sexual identity, plays a huge
role in how people feel that they

00:27:57.873 --> 00:28:02.180
belong here on campus, so
what have we done about that?

00:28:02.180 --> 00:28:05.359
We pilot what we call a small
wins approach to change,

00:28:05.359 --> 00:28:07.770
it's hard to change the whole culture.

00:28:07.770 --> 00:28:09.001
So we take bits of it, and

00:28:09.001 --> 00:28:13.060
we try to evolve it in some of
the ways that I talked about today.

00:28:13.060 --> 00:28:18.178
So one of the things we did was
last year we said we want all of

00:28:18.178 --> 00:28:23.940
our resources developing new cases
to focus on leaders of color.

00:28:25.110 --> 00:28:31.740
And it went from just 1% of our cases
being to adding 35 new cases last year.

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:35.238
And I heard one of the students
share in a course saying,

00:28:35.238 --> 00:28:38.289
this was the first time
when I was in my class, and

00:28:38.289 --> 00:28:42.250
there was a protagonist of color
talking about their startup.

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:46.929
Not talking solely about being
a protagonist of color or like

00:28:46.929 --> 00:28:52.690
an entrepreneur of color, but being one
and sharing about their amazing startup.

00:28:52.690 --> 00:28:59.476
So, it really can go a long way and
having our students feel like yes,

00:28:59.476 --> 00:29:06.734
they truly can thrive regardless of
how they look or where they come from.

00:29:06.734 --> 00:29:12.404
We've also thought about how we can
help our faculty create more inclusive

00:29:12.404 --> 00:29:18.535
classroom experiences and this just ended
the student panel was just this week.

00:29:18.535 --> 00:29:21.915
So we did a couple of things,
first two years ago,

00:29:21.915 --> 00:29:26.584
we launched a training called,
sensitive topics in the classroom,

00:29:26.584 --> 00:29:31.496
where we talked about how to manage
sensitive topics that come up through

00:29:31.496 --> 00:29:35.523
current events or
case studies that could inadvertently or

00:29:35.523 --> 00:29:41.185
comment that could inadvertently cause
someone to feel like they didn't belong.

00:29:41.185 --> 00:29:45.089
And then this year what they did
was we went a step further, first,

00:29:45.089 --> 00:29:49.554
we had a panel and invited faculty to
attend of three faculty who shared their

00:29:49.554 --> 00:29:54.512
experiences of both creating an inclusive
classroom but also dealing with pushback

00:29:54.512 --> 00:29:58.940
against their identities in
the classroom and what they did.

00:29:58.940 --> 00:30:03.923
And then just this week, Wal Torres
who works in our admissions curated

00:30:03.923 --> 00:30:09.240
a panel of force five students who shared
their experiences in the classroom

00:30:09.240 --> 00:30:14.308
when it wasn't inclusive or shared
how amazing it is when professors did

00:30:14.308 --> 00:30:19.880
certain things to have their
identities be welcome in the classroom.

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:24.057
So again, how do we as a business school,
as a leading business school,

00:30:24.057 --> 00:30:28.205
create environments where everyone
can equally see that they thrive?

00:30:28.205 --> 00:30:33.335
We had a conversation with staff
on Thursday with Brian Cooke

00:30:33.335 --> 00:30:39.877
who works in our institutional research
and Ayodele Thomas who is the head for

00:30:39.877 --> 00:30:44.827
staff diversity, equity inclusion for
all of Stanford.

00:30:44.827 --> 00:30:49.059
Where we wanted to tell them we're
going to share these survey results

00:30:49.059 --> 00:30:53.010
with you and go through them with you so
everyone understands.

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:57.939
We're not hiding the data, we're sharing
the data so that we can all think about

00:30:57.939 --> 00:31:02.450
ways that we can reduce the barriers for
people feeling like they belong.

00:31:04.430 --> 00:31:07.550
And lastly, we've been looking
at the curriculum we do,

00:31:07.550 --> 00:31:10.620
I don't know if it has anyone
been to building momentum?

00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:15.449
So it was originally designed for
women alums entrepreneurs

00:31:15.449 --> 00:31:20.289
to help them succeed in
the ecosystem of entrepreneurship.

00:31:20.289 --> 00:31:24.545
And then in 2021 pivoted it thinking,
well, we're online,

00:31:24.545 --> 00:31:29.117
why don't we take advantage of that,
instead of being just for women.

00:31:29.117 --> 00:31:35.000
The idea was to advance diversity and
to give access to the amazing curriculum

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:40.178
offered here at the business school
to anyone who was able to log on.

00:31:40.178 --> 00:31:45.085
And as you can see, there were 355 people
who attended and there were requirements

00:31:45.085 --> 00:31:50.000
for registering, it wasn't just if
you were interested in hearing.

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:54.838
And 62% of them ended up being non alums,
which was really great for

00:31:54.838 --> 00:31:59.922
us to share our good work, and
50% of our attendees were either Black or

00:31:59.922 --> 00:32:03.440
Latino, US based attendees, right.

00:32:03.440 --> 00:32:07.880
So thinking about a curriculum,
a classroom experience, our staff,

00:32:07.880 --> 00:32:11.432
trying to debunk the myths that
it's fine the way it is and

00:32:11.432 --> 00:32:15.650
figuring out how we can be proactive and
creating more inclusion and

00:32:15.650 --> 00:32:19.480
more to be proud of here
at the business school.

00:32:19.480 --> 00:32:24.054
If you haven't read it,
it's on our website, if you just look for

00:32:24.054 --> 00:32:28.795
a diversity equity inclusion report,
we have six priorities that we

00:32:28.795 --> 00:32:33.840
work on here and we've been working on for
the past three years.

00:32:33.840 --> 00:32:37.176
One to increase the diversity
of our Stanford community,

00:32:37.176 --> 00:32:40.854
two to create an inclusive classroom and
learning experience,

00:32:40.854 --> 00:32:44.140
create an inclusive and
welcome main campus community.

00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:50.836
Support new research efforts, especially
around diversity, equity inclusion,

00:32:50.836 --> 00:32:55.396
share our good ideas with the world as
with the conference I shared about.

00:32:55.396 --> 00:32:58.651
And then we've integrated
our action plan for

00:32:58.651 --> 00:33:03.242
racial equity into the core report
with the last priority which is

00:33:03.242 --> 00:33:07.764
to address racial and
inequality in our community and society.

00:33:07.764 --> 00:33:12.942
So while we still have a lot of work
left to go, we're going to keep

00:33:12.942 --> 00:33:18.025
trying to debunk these myths and
install nudges and rituals so

00:33:18.025 --> 00:33:23.125
that we can, not only for
ourselves the student experience.

00:33:23.125 --> 00:33:27.529
But hopefully as that saying goes,
change lives, change the world,

00:33:27.529 --> 00:33:32.680
change organizations, change the world
hopefully by debunking the myths we will

00:33:32.680 --> 00:33:37.851
create more opportunities for people to
truly achieve their fullest potential.

00:33:37.851 --> 00:33:43.408
[MUSIC]

